1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT =Karin Andersdotter, i Dingelvik=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Anders Persson, i Dyndevik and Karin Mårtensdotter Dingelvik 
2 CONT * At Skifors, in the border between the homestead Kråkeviken herstädes and Svartkärn in Laxarby parish, are brickworks and qvarn, belonging to the owners of Gustafsfors ironworks. Skifors was owned in 1563 by wife Karin at Dingelv
2 CONC ik.
2 CONT * Old Steneby" part 2 pages 351 and 763.
2 CONT *  [https://archive.org/details/arkivkopia.se-runeberg-ladal/page/n69/mode/2up?q=Svenske '''Page N69 (7) Dyngowiik was written 1449. was then owned by the armor Sven Jonsson, and thus ancient farm. See one of him published Morning
2 CONC -Giftvobref at Grinstad parish, N: o 8. The same Sven Jonsson is mentioned in a letter on 10 ebr. 1494 (printed by Eilljegren after the original in v. Engeström Library, 1819. page 41). Published by Magnus Bonde's widow Kadrin Ing
2 CONC warsdotter. Was owned by the well-to-do Mrs. Karin, who held both Dingelviks farms. Eästviken, Åsen, Högen, Hölgerud, Sea, Bågen and others. In 1577 Nils Månsson at Bro was here: 1664 Mrs. Margareta Svenske: 1783 Hofjunkare Reuter
2 CONC : 1809 Ryttmä-Staren Sam. Kafle: 1827 Carl Pet. Åberg: 1845 The Crown County Man in Näs hardened by Wermland J. E. Wennerlöf: 18-17 Baronen S: t Gyr and Countess Ramel, born eewenhaupt. source: Beskrifning öfver grefskapet Dal.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://forum.rotter.se/index.php?topic=65483.10;wap2 Forum Rotter]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel Wallgren : In Valbo hårad's dombok 1622 (Edestam's summaries) you can read the following: Sven Eriksson, an old man of about 100 years old, born in Nordahls district in Torbjörnebyn and now living in Solberga in Högsäter s
2 CONC n in Valbo district, testified that in Gustav I's time he bought a hall. Anders Pedersson at Dyndevik [Dingelvik, Stenby sn, Vedbo hd, Gabriel's note], Säter's farm in Ör sn by 2 tax farmers Lars Katt and Olof Katt [Olof Katt in S
2 CONC äter, Ör sn, landscape deeds 1577 and 1578 according to Pähr Skrede, Gothenburg] for 80 mk örtige, and those and their heirs who sold it shall own it, but clean it up man by man and shift for:te Anders Persson's manger wellb. Nils
2 CONC  Månsson to Broo with hall. well Brun Jöransson and since then it has been under salvation and still is. But he does not know how the same Sæter came to salvation land from treasure.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils Månsson is identical to the Nils Månsson [Roos af Hjelmsäter] who registered at Bro in Bro sn, Näs härad, Värmland county and who was married to a granddaughter (not the daughter of Anders Persson) [see about this in JE Almqv
2 CONC ist, Herrgårdarna i Sweden during the Reformation period 1523-1611, Stockholm 1960, pp. 334f; cf. aa p 332.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Skrede and I have explained the circumstance that Brun Göransson is mentioned in the dombok notice in 1622 with the fact that his wife was also in some way one of Anders Persson's heirs, possibly married to a sister of Nils Månsso
2 CONC n's wife
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://arkivkopia.se/bok/runeberg-ladal Arkivkopia Runeberg]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dingelvik norre 1 qvarn. D:o south 1 saw. Högen 1 d:o. Læstviken 1 qvarn. Katrineholm ironworks at S. Dingelvik.Rå och Rör. y$ Gesslanda. 's Jusketorp.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Skrefs Atlebyn and Atlebön in the 1550s and 1570s in the tithe lengths.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Called Prayer by the common people.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Named Nolbyn in 1605 and was then recorded as a church plot. Rlef not assigned number of homes before 1635, under the title church homes.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) Commonly called Relen.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5) Called the old seat farm in 1681 and was then owned by the major Sv. Liedeman.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6) Now called Kristineberg. In 1681 it also bore the name of an old manor house. Owned then by wife Karin Svenske: 1683 by Ingehorg Silfversvärd. Yar Linnart Svinhufvud's estate in 1664. Bought from the provost Nils Hessel-gren in
2 CONC  1697 by the county chief J. Leijonbjelke for 510 r:dr curant, d. eh. 765 dalers silver coins. Built in 1805 by the provost L. Wallstedt; 1850 by commission surveyor H. Marin. m. fl.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7) Skrefs Dyngowiik 1449. Owned then by the squire Sven Jonsson, and thus an ancient manor. See a morning gift letter issued by him at Grinstad parish, No. 8. The same Sven Jonsson is mentioned in a letter on 10 February. 1494 (pr
2 CONC inted by Lilljegren after the original in v. Engeströmska library, 1819. page 41). published by Magnus Bonde's widow Kadrin Ingwarsdotter. Owned in 1563 by the well-to-do wife Karin, who owned both Dingelviks farms. Lästviken, Åse
2 CONC n, Högen, Hölgerud, Hafden, Bågen, etc. fl. In 1577, Nils Månsson at Bro was the owner of this: 1664 wife Margareta Svenske: 1783 court junior Reuter: 1809 horse master Sam. Tile: 1827 Carl Pet. Åberg: 1845 crown county in Näs dis
2 CONC trict of Wermland JF Wennerlöf: 18-17 baron St. Gyr and countess Ramel, born Lewenhaupt.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8) Has mostly had the same owner, as Norra Dingelvik. But owned in 1681 by Major Gregor. Ahlfclt, 1768 by the utility patron Sam. Chef.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9) Has also had the name Ekarebol. Called new manor in 1681. Owned then by Linnart Svinhufvud: 1783 by Sergeant Svinlmfvud: 1850 by C. Fr. Wow.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10) Old manor house. See X:o 7. Egdes 1664 by wife Margareta Svenske: 1686 by ensign Lars Svinhufvud: 1600 by Luiradshöfdingen Joh. Rho-din. who bought this manor from the latter for 500 riksdaler curant. 1 in later times belonged
2 CONC  to the owners of Billingsholm ironworks.
2 CONT
2 CONT 11) See i\:o 7. Was in 1664 well-born Patrik Tbomson's seat farm; 1681 Mrs. Agneta S\renskes: *Häradshöfdingcn. the assessor de Freses; In 1809 the commissioner was
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.hembygd.se/steneby/plats/312850/text/57689 Great Ocean, by Association: Steneby Hembygdsförening]
2 CONT
2 CONT Stora Havden was the salvation home mentioned in the first land registers in 1535. In the 1560s, Stora Havden, like all other salvation farms in Steneby, was owned by Karin Mårtensdotter from Dingelvik. She was the widow of Anders
2 CONC  Persson and she was the granddaughter of the gunsmith Sven Jonsson in Dingelvik. The farm was farmed by a country dweller and the first named such was called "Mattis i Hafden" who lived here in the 1560s.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the turn of the 19th century, Stora Havden was owned by Brynte Eriksson, one of the "seven hopefuls". He was born in Håbol in 1778. Brynte was married to Maria Lisa Jansdotter, born in 1779, daughter of Jan Segolsson in Gråsjö,
2 CONC  Ärtemark. Their daughter Maria Catharina, married to her cousin Peter Eriksson from Ulfsbyn, became the next owner of Stora Havden. Peter was very musical, which was passed down to later generations. After his death in 1865, Stor
2 CONC a Havden was sold to Saugbrugsforeningen in Fredrikshald. They leased the farm to August Hansson from Taxviken, born in 1845 and married to the daughter of Peter and Maria Catharina, Anna Greta Petersdotter. They had children Melk
2 CONC er, twins Albert and Carl, Bernhard and daughter Maria who died when she was 4 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1892, the men's building burned down and a new one similar to the old one was then built. August Hansson died in 1913 and his wife in 1920. The son Albert Hansson took over the lease. He lived in Åsen and farmed both farms. In 
2 CONC 1938 he handed over the lease to his son Henrik Hansson. After a fire in the barn in 1945, the Saugbrugsforeningen sold part of the farm to Henrik Hansson. He was married to Hildur née Jakobsson and they have five daughters. Nowad
2 CONC ays, youngest daughter Elisabeth lives in Stora Havden with her family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See "Old Steneby" part 2 pages 351 and 763.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dingelvik Dingelvik, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dingelvik is a place in Bengtsfors municipality , Västra Götaland county , located in Steneby parish . The development was defined by Statistics Norway as a small town in 1990 and was deregistered as such in 2020 due to the fact t
2 CONC hat the number of residents was below 50.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dingelvik Farm'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm in Dingelvik has been known since the Middle Ages, and the squire Sven Jonsson (half lily) (died around 1484), signed himself to Dingelvik . The farm was inherited within the Halsten Petersson family , and passed to the o
2 CONC lder Forstenas family (ram's head), and after a division into Norra and Södra Dingelvik, during the 17th century, northern Dingelvik was owned by the Svinhufvud family , while southern Dingelvik was owned by the Ahlefeldt family .
2 CONT
2 CONT A man building on Södra Dingelvik was built around 1848-1850 by the then owner Baron Henry de Reverony St. Cyr.
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm is today owned by the Kling family
2 CONT
