1 NOTE {geni:about_me} --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Borgmästare i Brätte, Vänersborg
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Segol Svensson Wallman (i Brätte)
2 CONT Born in 1590 - Brätte, Vassända-Naglum (P)
2 CONT Deceased in 1660 - Brätte, Vassända-Naglum (P), aged 70 years old
2 CONT
2 CONT https://gw.geneanet.org/karosenius?lang=en&p=segol+svensson&n=wallman+i+bratte
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses and children
2 CONT
2 CONT With Elisabet ? with
2 CONT # Elisabeth Segolsdotter Wallman †1657
2 CONT
2 CONT With Jonetta Balram to Pennington ca 1585-1624 with
2 CONT # Gustaf Segolsson Wallman †1660
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the numerous members of the genus
2 CONT
2 CONT Wallman marques Lösö citizen Segold Svensson, who settled in Brätte, became mayor there in 1630 and was a member of the town council in 1638.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The city and folk life
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known about what the small market town of Brätte actually looked like. No depictions or city plans have been preserved. The information that can be gleaned from contemporary written material also does not provide much 
2 CONC to hand. A late description of the city is given by the provost Harald Ullenius at the beginning of the 18th century: "Brätte was a beautiful city until Wenersborg was thought of. Still called the old town, as well as the market, 
2 CONC which is held annually on September 8, Brätte market. Torghällen is still there, and the remains of a bunch of stone streets can be seen. The ring wall that was around the town's wooden church still looks very rough. The cemetery 
2 CONC or grave site has been south of the city, and there are still some preserved gravestones, but no fence around it".
2 CONT
2 CONT What emerged from the archaeological excavations is that Brätte did not have a town plan drawn up in advance, but the buildings had to grow gradually. Cobbled streets have meandered among a cluster of houses, scattered on the slop
2 CONC e from Grytåsberget down towards the harbour. The houses have by all accounts been of a simpler kind, one-story log cabins with turf roofs. Next to the place where it is believed that the square was located, there is a preserved c
2 CONC ellar that may have belonged to the town's council house. The city's "coffin" (prison) was probably located in this basement. That one had one can be inferred from a dom book entry from 1631 which reveals a dramatic event.
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout the spring of 1631, the young woman Karin Clausdotter had tried to hide the fact that she was expecting a child. The fact that she looked forward to the child's birth with worry and without joy was because she and the c
2 CONC hild's father, Börje Hansson, were not married. Also, they were cousins. At this time, sexual intercourse between unmarried people and especially not so close relatives was not tolerated. It was called foul play and could be sever
2 CONC ely punished according to the law.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Karin felt that the time for delivery was approaching, she slipped away and gave birth to her child alone. A few days later, on June 2, the body of an infant was found floating in Vassbottenviken. When several people, despite
2 CONC  Karin's caution, knew about her pregnancy, she was immediately suspected of having murdered her newborn child.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the interrogations that followed in the town's council house, the despairing Karin was pressed hard by some of Brätte's most powerful people: the mayor Segol Svensson, the vicar Sven Jonae and the chaplain Olof Torstani. In add
2 CONC ition, some of the town's bourgeois wives came and tried to get Karin to tell the truth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Karin finally confessed to being solely guilty. The terrible crime she committed, she had, as she said herself, been "incited and driven to do so by the devil". No one would have known what she had done and even the child's father
2 CONC  would have been unaware as he was elsewhere at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the interrogation, Karin was locked in the basement of the council house, awaiting her verdict. In order to prevent her from escaping, they also locked both of her legs with a strong iron rod. During the night, however, Kari
2 CONC n somehow managed to free her legs from the irons, after which she was able to push up some planks in the basement ceiling. Through the narrow opening she had managed to create in the ceiling, Karin pushed her way up from the base
2 CONC ment to the hall of the council house. There she then opened a window and fled in the night out of the city. To save herself from a presumed death sentence, she made her way west and across the then national border into the Norweg
2 CONC ian Bohuslän. After that, Karin Clausdotter's fate is unknown and her name no longer appears in the historical sources. The father, Börje Hansson, was convicted in November of the same year for unauthorized sexual intercourse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other public buildings that existed in the city include a hospital, a customs house and possibly a schoolhouse. Around the city stretched a fence with four toll gates: Kungsporten, Dalboporten, Stadsporten and Bommen. A mill has p
2 CONC robably been located by the river. Furthermore, the city has housed a number of trade stalls, workshops of various kinds, taverns and inn businesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT The main source of knowledge about the town's public life is the court registers, which extend between the years 1615 – 1642. Here you can find information about all kinds of disputes and business. Rådstuga was not infrequently he
2 CONC ld a few days after the free markets, and the cases then often concerned fights and defamation, which occurred in connection with the market. However, it was very rarely, judging by the court record, that any serious crimes were c
2 CONC ommitted. One of the exceptions was a simple accident, in 1618, which ended in death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils, a farmer from Artorp in Gärdhem parish, had come riding heavily drunk on one of the narrow streets in Brätte. It had been rainy and mud mixed with household waste and cattle dung floated on the streets. When Nils, in his dru
2 CONC nken state, rode at speed along the street, the street dirt splashed over a warehouse belonging to a visiting goldsmith from Mariestad. The goldsmith became enraged and called out to a jack who was nearby. The jackal, whose name w
2 CONC as Måns, was promised a daler if he wanted to catch up with Nils and give him a round of beatings for what he had done. Måns was not slow to listen to the offer and he went with the moves to defend himself threateningly towards Ni
2 CONC ls. They both started arguing loudly, whereupon Nils quickly pulled out a knife which he thrust into the chest of the jack. Severely wounded, Måns fell to the ground and died six weeks later from his injuries. On his deathbed, how
2 CONC ever, Måns admitted that he was the one who first rushed towards Nils and threatened him with his sword. The court then judged that Nils had acted out of necessity and that he could turn to the king to obtain clemency from the cha
2 CONC rge of murder.
2 CONT
2 CONT Disputes about plot boundaries and goods and more were frequent. As an example, a battle over a salt barrel can be mentioned, which ended somewhat differently:
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 1642, Jacob Fransson and Börje Finsel came before the council court in Brätte. They had come into conflict over a keg of salt that they both claimed. In their anger, they had scolded and showered each other with obsceni
2 CONC ties and insults. In front of the court, despite the earlier bitter words, Jacob and Börje managed to reconcile. In the presence of the witnesses, they agreed that the enmity would be forgotten and that neither of them would bring
2 CONC  up the matter again. In order for neither party to benefit before the other, the court decided that the salt barrel should be confiscated and donated to the city's hospital. In addition, it was decided that if Jacob and Börje wer
2 CONC e to break their promise and get into a fight again, only the one who started a possible fight would be judged.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many cases concern immorality of various kinds. The most common is so-called "mökränkning", both parties unmarried, which was usually punished with a fine of 40 marks. If one or both parties were married, so-called single or doubl
2 CONC e adultery, according to the law of Moses, was condemned to death. However, it was far from common that these death sentences were carried out.
2 CONT
2 CONT With regard to the occupations of the townspeople, it can be deduced from the 1639 census that 7 people operated "all kinds of trades", 8 people "brucka spezerij vthi boder", 16 people made a living from "woodcutting", 12 people o
2 CONC wned schooners, 6 people owned rafts, 2 were goldsmiths, 3 were tailors, 3 were shoemakers, 2 were carpenters, 1 was a blacksmith, 5 people ran brewery operations, 3 were bakers, 3 were butchers while 16 citizens made their living
2 CONC  from transport on the Edsvägen. In the same year as above, the town kept the following set of livestock: 80 cows, 22 horses, 15 mares, 15 heifers, 45 pigs, 68 sheep, 7 rice cutters (goats), 1 bull, 2 oxen and 6 steers.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
