1 NOTE {geni:about_me} alt b. 8 June 1636 Göteborg, Vastra Gotaland, Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Annika Andersdotter Friend BIRTH	1638 DEATH	1724 (aged 85–86) BURIAL	Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/134378327/annika-friend
2 CONT
2 CONT Children Jonas Friend
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude Friend Archer unknown–1738
2 CONT
2 CONT Brigitta Friend Cox 1657 – unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Photo	 Andrew Nilsson Friend 1659–1741
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Catherine Friend Dalbo 1661–1721
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Friend Cox 1663 – unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes nilsson Friend 1666–1737
2 CONT
2 CONT Susannah Friend Enochsen 1670 – unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel Friend 1674 – unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Lawrence Friend 1676 – unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbara Nilsdotter Friend Longacre 1678–1739
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Friend Nicholas 1682 – unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Friend Urian 1684 – unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils (Nicholas) and Anna Andersdotter were married about November 1656 in Upland, she was the daughter of Anders Andersson and Christina Goolbrant (Guldbrand?). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils and Anna had 11 (10 surviving) children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Brigitta (Brita) Nilsdotter, b. 1657, who married John Cock or Cox.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anders (Andrew) Nilsson, b. 1659, who married Rebecca Helm
2 CONT
2 CONT Carin (Catharine) Nilsdotter, b. 1661, who married Olof Dalbo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Maria (Mary) Nilsdotter, b. 1663, who married Gabriel Cock or Cox
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes (John) Nilsson, b. 1666, who married Anna (Hendricksdotter) Coleman
2 CONT
2 CONT Susannah Nilsdotter, b. Abt. 1670,who married Enoch Enochson
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Nilsdotter, b. Abt. 1672, who married Amos Nichols
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel Nilsson , b. Abt. 1674, who married Maria Van Culen
2 CONT
2 CONT Lars (Lawrence) Nilsson, b. Abt. 1676, who married Sarah Jaquet
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbara Nilsdotter, b. 1676, who married ?
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna survived her husband till about the year 1724, when she died at the advanced age of 106 years.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT It is probable that Anna Andersdotter arrived in New Sweden on the "Mercurius" in March 1656. (See Johnson, "Swedish Settlements", 635) The list for this ship included two families headed by an Anders: Anders Larsson with wife and
2 CONC  five children and Anders Jacobsson with wife, maid-servant and three children. ("Id.", 725-26). Source - "The Family of Nils Larsson Frande (Friend)" copyright 1988 by Peter S. Craig. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Neal's death in 1689, Anna and two sons sold 182 acres embracing what is now a central part of Chester, PA. The deed states: 
2 CONT
2 CONT KNOW all men that we Anne ffriend widow and Relict of Neal Lawsa als ffriend late of Chester als Upland deced. Andrew ffriend Son & heir of the said Neals & Anne for the Consideration of one hundred & ten pounds Currant Silver mon
2 CONC y & pay of this Province to us paid by David Lloyd of Philahelphia.......... Have given granted Enfecffed (invested?)................ 
2 CONT
2 CONT plantation and Tract of land Situate in Chester als Upland afoiresaid formerly in the tenure of the said Neals Lawsa als ffriend his heirs & Assigns forever by a Patent or Grant therof made & Executed under the hand & seal of ffra
2 CONC ncis Lovelace Esqr. Governor General under the late Duke of York & of all his territ. In America bearing Date the Nineth day of April 1669........... 
2 CONT
2 CONT The mark of Ann A ffriend SEAL 
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew ffriend SEAL 
2 CONT
2 CONT his mark 
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes F ffriend SEAL 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Deed recorded 24 September 1766 in Chester County, Pennsylvania. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Deed Book O, p. 338). Source - "Indian Blood", Evelyn Guard Olsen, pg. 18. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In December, 1687 Chester County Court yielded an indictment against "Annie, Neale's widow" for keeping and harboring dogs that worries and kills her neighbors hogs, as also for detaining in her service one Indian boy Chato, who w
2 CONC ith the said dogs have been found to worry and kill the neighbor's hogs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Annie's son Anders (Andrew) became bound to the King and Governor in the amount of 20 Pounds for the Indian boy Chato's good abareing (behavior). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 October, 1689, both Andrew and Mounce Lock acknowledged a Deed to "Anne Friend, widow, and her two sons, John and Andrew "for a plantation and p'misses Conteining 300 acres lying by ye River Delaware in Ridley Township" (Pennsyl
2 CONC vania). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In October, 1690 Anne Friend (Anna Andersdotter), and two of her children were sued for scandal and defamation but won out over the prosecutor, Mons Peterson Stacket and his son Peter Peterson Stacket. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1693 the tax list for Chester County, Pennsylvania showed "Widow Neales" residing in Ridley Township. The May, 1693 shows "Nils Frende's widow". 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The December 1687 Chester County Court yielded an indictment against "Annie, Neales' widow for keeping and harboring dogs that worries and kills her neighbor's hogs, as also for detaining in her service one Indian boy named Chato 
2 CONC who with the said dog have been found to worry and kill the neighbor's hogs." "The said Anne Neales [sic] came into Court and before her arraignment declared that the said dog belonged to Peter Cox [Peter Cock, her apparent grands
2 CONC on] but, because she was not willing to plead to the said indictment, puts herself upon the mercy of the King and Governor and craves to be admitted to a fine, upon which she is fined ten shillings and to pay all court charges wit
2 CONC h sheriffs and clerk's fees, etc." 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "Andrew Friend became bound to the King and Governor in twenty pounds for the Indian boy Chato's good abareing [abearance, behavior] toward all the King's Leidge People." (CCR, 1:115)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: Page 12....The Family of Nils Larsson Friend by Dr. Peter Stebbins Craig
2 CONT ----
